\details{
\code{"auto"}, the default, is resolved by \code{fb_select()} against the projection
structure gate: \code{"projection"} where the reference model's structure is
one the provider can project, and \code{"full"} where it is not. The
resolution is recorded on the result and on its certificate, so a reader
can see which decision ran and why. Most of what this package fits --
known-covariance genomic and pedigree terms, unstructured and
factor-analytic covariances, autoregressive fields, smooths -- is red at
that gate, and a default of \code{"projection"} meant those models refused
only after a reference model had been sampled. Naming \code{"projection"}
explicitly on a red gate still refuses, by name, with the feature that
failed.

The three methods themselves answer three different questions.

\code{"projection"} projects the reference posterior onto each submodel and
the submodel's expected log predictive density is assessed on the same
folds the rest of the selection uses, with the standard error aggregated
at the resampling unit rather than at the row. Protected terms and the
base model's random terms are forced into every submodel.

\code{"full"} returns the reference posterior with no selection, for a user who
wants shrinkage without a decision.

\code{"posterior_threshold"} marks a candidate retained when the posterior
probability that the magnitude of its coefficient exceeds \code{effect_size} is
above \code{threshold}. It is a decision rule and nothing more: a continuous
shrinkage prior puts no mass at zero, so the quantity thresholded is not a
posterior inclusion probability and must not be read as one. \code{effect_size}
is on the scientific scale of the candidate column and has no default --
what counts as a practically important effect is a property of the
science, not of the software.

The three rules operate on the elpd curve by submodel size. \code{"min"} takes
the best size. \code{"one_se"} takes the smallest size whose paired loss
against the best is within one standard error of that paired difference.
\code{"tolerance"} takes the smallest size losing less than \code{tolerance} nats
per unit against the reference model.

The three \code{stability_} settings ask for subsample stability of the
penalised optimiser's support (Meinshausen and Buhlmann 2010,
\doi{10.1111/j.1467-9868.2010.00740.x}). Each of \code{stability_subsamples}
subsamples holds half of the units of \code{stability_unit}, drawn without
replacement after \code{set.seed(seed)}, and the penalised path is walked on it
over the same \code{s = lambda / lambda_max} grid as the cross-validated path. A
column's frequency at a point is the share of the subsamples whose
solution holds it there, and the column is stable when its largest
frequency at or above the chosen tuning point reaches \code{stability_cutoff}.
Under the exchangeability condition of their Theorem 1, the expected
number of falsely stable columns is at most \verb{q^2 / ((2 cutoff - 1) p)},
where \code{q} is the average number of columns a subsample selects in that
region and \code{p} the number of penalised columns; the result reports both.
The stable set is a read-out beside the optimiser's chosen support and
does not replace it. It runs wherever the penalised optimiser runs, as the
workflow or as the benchmark arm of \code{benchmark = TRUE}, and costs
\code{stability_subsamples} penalised paths, which \code{plan = TRUE} states.
}
